			<div class="content" id="post-content-4139269">
									

<p>This article’s tragedy is that it’s a boring story about a magic mecha in space. The article clearly hews after the super robot sub-genre of mecha. Unfortunately, the clinical tone required in an SCP strips out the style and flashiness of the action sequences and replaces them with a dull sterility. I don’t get any frisson from learning about the exoskeleton’s cool weapons or how they work, because you fall back on thaumaturgy as a narrative crutch to let you have all these cool weapons without bothering to talk about them in any depth. Katrina Kuang and Nobody aren’t developed at all, and the setting—a <em>space station near Mars</em>—has zero consequence on the story at all. You could have implemented some cool zero-G sequences or utilized the geometry of the O’Neill cylinder for an escape sequence, but the space station reads no differently from a generic metropolis set twenty years into Broken Masquerade. The article feels like one big missed opportunity.</p>

